          "heading": "Planning reliable water services",
          "content": "# Unit 1: Planning reliable water services\n\n## Engineering problem\n\nUnderstand service populations, demand variation, seasonal sources, and water-service reliability as a coupled social and engineering problem.\n\n**System context.** A regional utility and its engineering consultant must keep household access, source protection, operating budget, plant controls, maintenance, public-health risk and the downstream environment in a single decision record. This unit focuses on equity and governance, source quality versus quantity, demand uncertainty, operator response. A calculated capacity or ideal removal percentage only answers a narrow question; it never certifies operation.\n\n## What to carry into the next unit\n\nTreat assumptions as named data rather than invisible constants. Where a model omits fluctuating inflow, temperature, maintenance, unserved customers or instrument uncertainty, state the omission before drawing conclusions. The case studies show how apparently plausible outputs can fail after a changed boundary condition.\n\n## Lesson sequence\n\n- Lesson 1: [Service levels and the catchment-to-consumer boundary](lessons/unit-01-lesson-01.md)\n- Lesson 2: [Population forecasts and time-varying water demand](lessons/unit-01-lesson-02.md)\n- Lesson 3: [Source selection, seasonal yield and intake vulnerability](lessons/unit-01-lesson-03.md)\n- Lesson 4: [Water safety, resilience and uncertain decisions](lessons/unit-01-lesson-04.md)\n\n## Operating envelope\n\nThe linked lessons address Service levels and the catchment-to-consumer boundary; Population forecasts and time-varying water demand; Source selection, seasonal yield and intake vulnerability; Water safety, resilience and uncertain decisions. Separate predictable day-to-day variation from rare hazardous events. A well-built model should expose its domain: the range of flow, concentration, temperature and operating condition over which its assumptions are defensible. Utility investigations often require repeat observations during dry weather and storms, and distribution or collection networks need spatially distributed data rather than a single inlet measurement.\n\n## Structured investigation\n\nChoose one real or synthetic utility. Sketch a flow schematic with three explicitly labeled boundaries: incoming quantity and quality, transformed or stored inventory, and outgoing quantity and quality. Provide at least one mass or energy balance, justify the time horizon, and make a quantitative uncertainty or sensitivity comparison. The main practical limitation to discuss is settlement population is only a planning assumption and may omit unconnected residents.\n\n### Conditions to challenge\n\n- Settlement population is only a planning assumption and may omit unconnected residents.\n- Climate and source quality can change faster than demographic forecasts.\n- A peak multiplier cannot be borrowed without considering service type and peak definition."
