          "heading": "Distribution systems, storage and pumping",
          "content": "# Unit 2: Distribution systems, storage and pumping\n\n## Engineering problem\n\nLink conservation of mass and energy to the behavior of pumps, pipes and tanks under realistic operating constraints.\n\n**System context.** A regional utility and its engineering consultant must keep household access, source protection, operating budget, plant controls, maintenance, public-health risk and the downstream environment in a single decision record. This unit focuses on mass conservation, hydraulic gradients, energy and motor selection, time-dependent storage. A calculated capacity or ideal removal percentage only answers a narrow question; it never certifies operation.\n\n## What to carry into the next unit\n\nTreat assumptions as named data rather than invisible constants. Where a model omits fluctuating inflow, temperature, maintenance, unserved customers or instrument uncertainty, state the omission before drawing conclusions. The case studies show how apparently plausible outputs can fail after a changed boundary condition.\n\n## Lesson sequence\n\n- Lesson 1: [Continuity and network topology](lessons/unit-02-lesson-01.md)\n- Lesson 2: [Pipe energy losses and pressure limits](lessons/unit-02-lesson-02.md)\n- Lesson 3: [Pump curves, efficiency and system operating points](lessons/unit-02-lesson-03.md)\n- Lesson 4: [Service reservoirs, transients and emergency storage](lessons/unit-02-lesson-04.md)\n\n## Operating envelope\n\nThe linked lessons address Continuity and network topology; Pipe energy losses and pressure limits; Pump curves, efficiency and system operating points; Service reservoirs, transients and emergency storage. Separate predictable day-to-day variation from rare hazardous events. A well-built model should expose its domain: the range of flow, concentration, temperature and operating condition over which its assumptions are defensible. Utility investigations often require repeat observations during dry weather and storms, and distribution or collection networks need spatially distributed data rather than a single inlet measurement.\n\n## Structured investigation\n\nChoose one real or synthetic utility. Sketch a flow schematic with three explicitly labeled boundaries: incoming quantity and quality, transformed or stored inventory, and outgoing quantity and quality. Provide at least one mass or energy balance, justify the time horizon, and make a quantitative uncertainty or sensitivity comparison. The main practical limitation to discuss is water density and viscosity are approximated as constant.\n\n### Conditions to challenge\n\n- Water density and viscosity are approximated as constant.\n- An imposed Darcy friction factor does not respond to temperature or turbulence.\n- A quadratic pump model can misrepresent efficiency and cavitation."
