          "heading": "Drinking-water treatment processes",
          "content": "# Unit 3: Drinking-water treatment processes\n\n## Engineering problem\n\nExamine treatment as multiple verified physical and chemical barriers rather than a single removal-number claim.\n\n**System context.** A regional utility and its engineering consultant must keep household access, source protection, operating budget, plant controls, maintenance, public-health risk and the downstream environment in a single decision record. This unit focuses on hazard-screening methods, colloid destabilization, separation mechanisms, resistance and breakthrough. A calculated capacity or ideal removal percentage only answers a narrow question; it never certifies operation.\n\n## What to carry into the next unit\n\nTreat assumptions as named data rather than invisible constants. Where a model omits fluctuating inflow, temperature, maintenance, unserved customers or instrument uncertainty, state the omission before drawing conclusions. The case studies show how apparently plausible outputs can fail after a changed boundary condition.\n\n## Lesson sequence\n\n- Lesson 1: [Source characterization and treatment trains](lessons/unit-03-lesson-01.md)\n- Lesson 2: [Coagulation and flocculation mechanics](lessons/unit-03-lesson-02.md)\n- Lesson 3: [Sedimentation and particle removal](lessons/unit-03-lesson-03.md)\n- Lesson 4: [Filtration, breakthrough and backwash](lessons/unit-03-lesson-04.md)\n\n## Operating envelope\n\nThe linked lessons address Source characterization and treatment trains; Coagulation and flocculation mechanics; Sedimentation and particle removal; Filtration, breakthrough and backwash. Separate predictable day-to-day variation from rare hazardous events. A well-built model should expose its domain: the range of flow, concentration, temperature and operating condition over which its assumptions are defensible. Utility investigations often require repeat observations during dry weather and storms, and distribution or collection networks need spatially distributed data rather than a single inlet measurement.\n\n## Structured investigation\n\nChoose one real or synthetic utility. Sketch a flow schematic with three explicitly labeled boundaries: incoming quantity and quality, transformed or stored inventory, and outgoing quantity and quality. Provide at least one mass or energy balance, justify the time horizon, and make a quantitative uncertainty or sensitivity comparison. The main practical limitation to discuss is well-mixed, ideal reactor assumptions exclude short circuiting.\n\n### Conditions to challenge\n\n- Well-mixed, ideal reactor assumptions exclude short circuiting.\n- Particles in real sedimentation units are not identical discrete spheres.\n- Clean-bed permeability cannot predict breakthrough in loaded filters."
