          "heading": "Disinfection and distribution integrity",
          "content": "# Unit 4: Disinfection and distribution integrity\n\n## Engineering problem\n\nUnderstand contact, residuals, intrusion, chemical byproducts and operational verification without pretending simple models certify safety.\n\n**System context.** A regional utility and its engineering consultant must keep household access, source protection, operating budget, plant controls, maintenance, public-health risk and the downstream environment in a single decision record. This unit focuses on pathogen barriers, residual behavior, chemical trade-offs, verification and responsibility. A calculated capacity or ideal removal percentage only answers a narrow question; it never certifies operation.\n\n## What to carry into the next unit\n\nTreat assumptions as named data rather than invisible constants. Where a model omits fluctuating inflow, temperature, maintenance, unserved customers or instrument uncertainty, state the omission before drawing conclusions. The case studies show how apparently plausible outputs can fail after a changed boundary condition.\n\n## Lesson sequence\n\n- Lesson 1: [Microbial barriers and contact-time fundamentals](lessons/unit-04-lesson-01.md)\n- Lesson 2: [Disinfectant residual decay and regrowth](lessons/unit-04-lesson-02.md)\n- Lesson 3: [Disinfection byproducts and competing objectives](lessons/unit-04-lesson-03.md)\n- Lesson 4: [Water safety plans and sanitary inspection](lessons/unit-04-lesson-04.md)\n\n## Operating envelope\n\nThe linked lessons address Microbial barriers and contact-time fundamentals; Disinfectant residual decay and regrowth; Disinfection byproducts and competing objectives; Water safety plans and sanitary inspection. Separate predictable day-to-day variation from rare hazardous events. A well-built model should expose its domain: the range of flow, concentration, temperature and operating condition over which its assumptions are defensible. Utility investigations often require repeat observations during dry weather and storms, and distribution or collection networks need spatially distributed data rather than a single inlet measurement.\n\n## Structured investigation\n\nChoose one real or synthetic utility. Sketch a flow schematic with three explicitly labeled boundaries: incoming quantity and quality, transformed or stored inventory, and outgoing quantity and quality. Provide at least one mass or energy balance, justify the time horizon, and make a quantitative uncertainty or sensitivity comparison. The main practical limitation to discuss is bulk decay excludes pipe-wall reaction.\n\n### Conditions to challenge\n\n- Bulk decay excludes pipe-wall reaction.\n- A disinfectant CT product is not independent pathogen verification.\n- Compliance targets vary by organism, jurisdiction and source water."
