          "content": "# Unit 5: Sewerage collection and wet-weather hydraulics\n\n## Engineering problem\n\nTrace wastewater from its sources through collection networks while retaining distinctions among sanitary flow, infiltration and storm runoff.\n\n**System context.** A regional utility and its engineering consultant must keep household access, source protection, operating budget, plant controls, maintenance, public-health risk and the downstream environment in a single decision record. This unit focuses on mass loading, open-channel hydraulics, rainfall response, failure prevention. A calculated capacity or ideal removal percentage only answers a narrow question; it never certifies operation.\n\n## What to carry into the next unit\n\nTreat assumptions as named data rather than invisible constants. Where a model omits fluctuating inflow, temperature, maintenance, unserved customers or instrument uncertainty, state the omission before drawing conclusions. The case studies show how apparently plausible outputs can fail after a changed boundary condition.\n\n## Lesson sequence\n\n- Lesson 1: [Wastewater loads, diurnal factors and characterization](lessons/unit-05-lesson-01.md)\n- Lesson 2: [Gravity sewer sizing and Manning hydraulics](lessons/unit-05-lesson-02.md)\n- Lesson 3: [Infiltration, inflow and wet-weather peaks](lessons/unit-05-lesson-03.md)\n- Lesson 4: [Sewer operation, corrosion and overflow prevention](lessons/unit-05-lesson-04.md)\n\n## Operating envelope\n\nThe linked lessons address Wastewater loads, diurnal factors and characterization; Gravity sewer sizing and Manning hydraulics; Infiltration, inflow and wet-weather peaks; Sewer operation, corrosion and overflow prevention. Separate predictable day-to-day variation from rare hazardous events. A well-built model should expose its domain: the range of flow, concentration, temperature and operating condition over which its assumptions are defensible. Utility investigations often require repeat observations during dry weather and storms, and distribution or collection networks need spatially distributed data rather than a single inlet measurement.\n\n## Structured investigation\n\nChoose one real or synthetic utility. Sketch a flow schematic with three explicitly labeled boundaries: incoming quantity and quality, transformed or stored inventory, and outgoing quantity and quality. Provide at least one mass or energy balance, justify the time horizon, and make a quantitative uncertainty or sensitivity comparison. The main practical limitation to discuss is full-pipe uniform flow excludes partial flow and upstream backwater.\n\n### Conditions to challenge\n\n- Full-pipe uniform flow excludes partial flow and upstream backwater.\n- Storm runoff coefficients are screening values, not sewer I/I measurements.\n- Industrial constituents and odor processes require dedicated characterization."
