          "content": "# Unit 6: Biological treatment and process control\n\n## Engineering problem\n\nUse mass balances and reaction-rate thinking to interpret primary, secondary and nutrient-removal treatment.\n\n**System context.** A regional utility and its engineering consultant must keep household access, source protection, operating budget, plant controls, maintenance, public-health risk and the downstream environment in a single decision record. This unit focuses on materials characterization, oxygen kinetics, microbial inventory, nutrient transformations. A calculated capacity or ideal removal percentage only answers a narrow question; it never certifies operation.\n\n## What to carry into the next unit\n\nTreat assumptions as named data rather than invisible constants. Where a model omits fluctuating inflow, temperature, maintenance, unserved customers or instrument uncertainty, state the omission before drawing conclusions. The case studies show how apparently plausible outputs can fail after a changed boundary condition.\n\n## Lesson sequence\n\n- Lesson 1: [Primary treatment and influent fractionation](lessons/unit-06-lesson-01.md)\n- Lesson 2: [BOD kinetics, oxygen demand and process time](lessons/unit-06-lesson-02.md)\n- Lesson 3: [Activated sludge loading and oxygen balance](lessons/unit-06-lesson-03.md)\n- Lesson 4: [Nitrification, denitrification and phosphorus removal](lessons/unit-06-lesson-04.md)\n\n## Operating envelope\n\nThe linked lessons address Primary treatment and influent fractionation; BOD kinetics, oxygen demand and process time; Activated sludge loading and oxygen balance; Nitrification, denitrification and phosphorus removal. Separate predictable day-to-day variation from rare hazardous events. A well-built model should expose its domain: the range of flow, concentration, temperature and operating condition over which its assumptions are defensible. Utility investigations often require repeat observations during dry weather and storms, and distribution or collection networks need spatially distributed data rather than a single inlet measurement.\n\n## Structured investigation\n\nChoose one real or synthetic utility. Sketch a flow schematic with three explicitly labeled boundaries: incoming quantity and quality, transformed or stored inventory, and outgoing quantity and quality. Provide at least one mass or energy balance, justify the time horizon, and make a quantitative uncertainty or sensitivity comparison. The main practical limitation to discuss is a bod rate constant depends on the test definition and temperature.\n\n### Conditions to challenge\n\n- A BOD rate constant depends on the test definition and temperature.\n- MLSS is not equivalent to active biomass.\n- Nutrient removal depends on actual redox zones, carbon and alkalinity."
