          "id": "sec-8-1",
          "secNumber": "8.1",
          "heading": "Plant operation, economics and resilience",
          "content": "# Unit 8: Plant operation, economics and resilience\n\n## Engineering problem\n\nPull the hydraulics, process units and public-health safeguards into a coherent operating and capital-investment plan.\n\n**System context.** A regional utility and its engineering consultant must keep household access, source protection, operating budget, plant controls, maintenance, public-health risk and the downstream environment in a single decision record. This unit focuses on instrument drift, financial sensitivity, compound hazards, investment staging. A calculated capacity or ideal removal percentage only answers a narrow question; it never certifies operation.\n\n## What to carry into the next unit\n\nTreat assumptions as named data rather than invisible constants. Where a model omits fluctuating inflow, temperature, maintenance, unserved customers or instrument uncertainty, state the omission before drawing conclusions. The case studies show how apparently plausible outputs can fail after a changed boundary condition.\n\n## Lesson sequence\n\n- Lesson 1: [Instrumentation, calibration and process data](lessons/unit-08-lesson-01.md)\n- Lesson 2: [Energy, lifecycle costs and affordability](lessons/unit-08-lesson-02.md)\n- Lesson 3: [Climate stress tests and emergency response](lessons/unit-08-lesson-03.md)\n- Lesson 4: [Integrated capstone: water and wastewater master plan](lessons/unit-08-lesson-04.md)\n\n## Operating envelope\n\nThe linked lessons address Instrumentation, calibration and process data; Energy, lifecycle costs and affordability; Climate stress tests and emergency response; Integrated capstone: water and wastewater master plan. Separate predictable day-to-day variation from rare hazardous events. A well-built model should expose its domain: the range of flow, concentration, temperature and operating condition over which its assumptions are defensible. Utility investigations often require repeat observations during dry weather and storms, and distribution or collection networks need spatially distributed data rather than a single inlet measurement.\n\n## Structured investigation\n\nChoose one real or synthetic utility. Sketch a flow schematic with three explicitly labeled boundaries: incoming quantity and quality, transformed or stored inventory, and outgoing quantity and quality. Provide at least one mass or energy balance, justify the time horizon, and make a quantitative uncertainty or sensitivity comparison. The main practical limitation to discuss is instrument accuracy is not identical to process uncertainty.\n\n### Conditions to challenge\n\n- Instrument accuracy is not identical to process uncertainty.\n- Price estimates exclude costs unless explicitly itemized.\n- Resilience can fail through shared infrastructure despite unit redundancy."
