          "content": "# Unit 8 \u00b7 Lesson 2: Energy, lifecycle costs and affordability\n\n## Learning outcomes\n\nBy the end of this lesson, explain **financial sensitivity** using a defensible system boundary; identify the measurements needed to test a conceptual calculation; and distinguish an illustrative formula from an engineering acceptance decision.\n\n## Core reasoning\n\nCompare capital expense, electricity, chemicals, staffing, replacement, residuals disposal and failure costs. Levelized unit cost requires a declared lifetime, discounting convention and produced rather than merely designed annual volume.\n\nUnlike a textbook exercise that starts by offering all required parameters, a real engineering team normally starts with incomplete measurements. The first analytical task is to decide which boundary encloses the operation being investigated. For energy, lifecycle costs and affordability, confirm whether the accounting period is hourly, daily, seasonal, or lifetime; whether the reported figures refer to flow, mass or concentration; and where streams enter, leave or are temporarily stored. If a reservoir is refilling, the local flow balance must include a storage term. If a laboratory result is a concentration, multiplying by the simultaneous volumetric flow gives a load; the two quantities answer different planning questions.\n\nData quality determines what can be inferred. Field instruments need locations and calibration dates. Laboratory measurements need sampling protocols, preservation details and timestamps. Maintenance logs and operator observations often explain anomalies that disappear from a monthly mean. When a parameter is only an estimate, calculate sensitivity before arguing for a larger structure or a more powerful pump. A high flow multiplier cannot rescue a mislabeled unit, and an efficient machine cannot remove contaminants that its process is not designed to target.\n\n## Worked engineering investigation\n\nAt 0.25 kWh/m\u00b3 and 15,000 m\u00b3/day, electrical use is 3,750 kWh/day. An energy tariff of $0.12/kWh produces $450/day before demand charges or other costs.\n\n**Reconstruction method.** Identify the known variables, write their units, and choose the minimum physical relations needed to reproduce the quoted result. Record whether the figure is a rate, a volume, or a concentration. Then calculate one counterfactual by changing an operational parameter without changing others. Interpret the direction of change in a sentence. Avoid using rounded display values in later computations: retain precision internally and round only when presenting a result.\n\n**Boundary check.** Ask what the example deliberately omits. For this lesson, omissions may include future demand, seasonal variation, upstream losses, biological variability, energy constraints or monitoring gaps, depending on the equipment. Label these rather than appending an unspecified \u201csafety factor.\u201d A missing constraint is not equivalent to random uncertainty: some constraints must be investigated directly before the calculation is relevant.\n\n## Field and design interpretation\n\nSeparate known utility bills from hypothetical efficiency savings and calculate the payback sensitivity to actual operating hours.\n\nAn engineering recommendation should connect evidence to an action. If available observations contradict the ideal model, investigate both the physical process and the measurement chain. Begin with obvious errors such as inconsistent units or a faulty sensor; then review omitted inflows, mixing, interference, temperature, asset condition and actual operating schedules. Keep the alternative explanations in the report so a different operator can repeat the reasoning.\n\n## Analytical comparison\n\nCompare this lesson with **Integrated capstone: water and wastewater master plan**. The two topics act on different parts of the same supply or wastewater pathway. One may change instantaneous hydraulic capacity while the other changes treatment efficacy, mass loading, environmental effect or operational reliability. Construct a diagram with a measured variable at each boundary, the source of that measurement, and the failure mode that might invalidate the result.\n\n## Critical-thinking assessment\n\n1. Which numerical measurement would most reduce the uncertainty in this lesson, and why is it preferable to a generic factor?\n2. What observation would falsify the assumed relationship while leaving the measured input unchanged?\n3. Which stakeholder is affected first if the simplified calculation is used as a final engineering decision?\n4. Produce a worked counterexample using different but physically plausible input values, and show all unit conversions.\n5. Explain one safety, compliance or environmental decision that this lesson cannot establish.\n\n## Practice notebook\n\nRecord a model sketch, input inventory, dimensional consistency check, sensitivity calculation and concise conclusion. State what would be needed to turn your notebook into a site-specific engineering submission."
